For a Laravel project to function properly on a Linux server, you need to set the correct file and directory permissions. Here are the recommended settings:
1. Set the Correct Directory Permissions

    Laravel requires the storage and bootstrap/cache directories to be writable.

    Set proper permissions using:

    sudo chown -R www-data:www-data storage bootstrap/cache
    sudo chmod -R 775 storage bootstrap/cache

        www-data is the web server user (for Apache/Nginx, adjust if needed).

        775 gives read, write, and execute permissions to the owner and group but only read and execute permissions to others.

2. Set the Correct File Permissions

    Ensure that other files and directories are set correctly:

    sudo find . -type f -exec chmod 644 {} \;
    sudo find . -type d -exec chmod 755 {} \;

        644 (for files) allows read and write for the owner, read-only for others.

        755 (for directories) allows read, write, and execute for the owner, read and execute for others.

3. Fix storage/logs/laravel.log Issues

    If Laravel logs cannot be written:

    sudo chmod -R 775 storage/logs

4. Ensure .env Security

    The .env file contains sensitive data. Set it to be readable only by the owner:

    sudo chmod 600 .env

5. Set Up SELinux (If Enabled)

    If your system uses SELinux, you might need to adjust the security context:

    sudo chcon -R -t httpd_sys_rw_content_t storage bootstrap/cache

By setting these permissions correctly, your Laravel project should function without permission-related issues. Let me know if you encounter any problems!
